# Hostile self-audit — bounded-face train endpoint fans

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Audited source:** `target_c_bounded_face_endpoint_fan_2026-09-12.md`  
**Disposition:** PASS at the stated proof-only scope; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.

This audit checks the finite structural claim that two complete trains sharing a
bounded stopping face cannot cross and that two trains cannot join the same pair of
bounded stopping faces. It does not promote the claim or address the outer face.

## 1. Crossing trains: prefix cut

Canonical train geometry supplies simple midpoint paths and at most one crossing for
two distinct trains. If they cross, the prefixes from their terminal occurrences on a
common bounded face to the crossing point, closed through the open face interior, form
a simple Jordan curve. Each prefix contains its terminal contact-edge crossing. The
curve is contained in the full source hull because strict-rhombus interiors and
bounded contact faces are contained there.

The Jordan interior therefore contains no endpoint of an active diameter edge: such an
endpoint is extreme in the source hull and cannot have an open neighborhood inside the
hull. Summed KKT equilibrium over the core vertices in the interior has no diameter
term and has contact terms from exactly the two selected train prefixes. Their positive
weights give

`epsilon_1 A_1 u_1 + epsilon_2 A_2 u_2 = 0`, with `A_i>0`.

Nonparallel projective directions cannot satisfy this equation. Parallel directions
give the piecewise transverse mixed flex from the audited pair-cut theorem. Hence the
crossing is impossible.

## 2. Disjoint trains: full two-face cut

If two trains join the same two bounded faces and are disjoint, their terminal
occurrences are in the noncrossing order. Joining corresponding occurrences through
the two open face interiors gives a Jordan curve in the source hull. The same KKT
shore sum has exactly one positive resultant from each train. Distinct projective
directions cannot cancel; equal projective directions are excluded by the audited
parallel pair-cut theorem. Thus a disjoint repeated pair is also impossible.

## 3. Singleton trains

For a singleton bounded-to-bounded train, the contact edge is internal to the source
hull because both incident faces are bounded. A sufficiently short transverse arc
through its midpoint supplies the same path representative and has exactly one
contact crossing. This handles a singleton paired with another train as long as the
canonical train normalization includes this standard representative. If a future
normalization omits it, the theorem must be restricted to non-singleton trains; no
singleton extension is silently assumed.

## 4. No hidden source deletion

Only the nonisolated positive-contact core enters the KKT sum and Section-57 rank
argument. Contact-isolated source points remain present and do not create diameter rows
in the core matrix. The hull-extreme argument is applied to actual active diameter
endpoints of the full source, so it does not require a full-source affine kernel
theorem.

## 5. Frontier limitation

The theorem gives simplicity and crossing-freeness for bounded-face endpoints. It does
not control repeated outer-face incidences, mixed outer/bounded pairs, local train
segments before they are globalized, or the aggregate detour
`sum_r(L_r-2d_r)`. Therefore the scalar half-disk witness and the canonical C-II
obstruction remain viable.

**Final audit verdict:** PASS as a proof-only structural candidate; C-II remains open.
